    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-qft-distribution-scaling-degree",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Scaling degree and local extension space",
        "deps": [
          "def-distribution"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-distribution": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. Brunetti and K. Fredenhagen, Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds, §5.1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math-ph/9903028"
            },
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, “Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory,” in Mathematical Aspects of Quantum Field Theories, ed. Damien Calaque and Thomas Strobl, Springer (2015), Section 7",
              "url": "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73875/1/95.pdf.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "K. J. Keller, Dimensional Regularization in Position Space and a Forest Formula for Regularized Epstein-Glaser Renormalization, dissertation, §I.1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1006.2148"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Fix an integer $n\\ge1$, put $U=\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and use the continuous complex-linear test-function pairing of [[def-distribution]]. For $t\\in\\mathcal D'(U)$ and $\\rho>0$, define its dilation at the origin by\n\n$$\\langle t_\\rho,\\varphi\\rangle:=\\rho^{-n}\\langle t,\\varphi(\\cdot/\\rho)\\rangle,\\qquad \\varphi\\in\\mathcal D(U).$$\n\nPositive dilations preserve $U$, so the displayed pairing is defined for every test function in $\\mathcal D(U)$. If $t$ is the regular distribution induced by a locally integrable function $g$, change of variables gives $\\langle t_\\rho,\\varphi\\rangle=\\int_U g(\\rho x)\\varphi(x)\\,dx$.\n\nConvergence $s_\\rho\\to0$ in $\\mathcal D'(U)$ as $\\rho\\downarrow0$ means $\\langle s_\\rho,\\varphi\\rangle\\to0$ for every fixed $\\varphi\\in\\mathcal D(U)$. The **scaling degree at the origin** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{sd}_0(t):=\\inf\\{\\omega\\in\\mathbb R:\\rho^\\omega t_\\rho\\to0\\text{ in }\\mathcal D'(U)\\}.$$\n\nThe infimum of the empty set is $+\\infty$; if every real $\\omega$ belongs to the set, the infimum is $-\\infty$. A finite scaling degree means a real value $\\delta\\in\\mathbb R$. For a distribution $T\\in\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^n)$ use the same dilation formula on tests in $\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^n)$. An **extension** of $t$ is a distribution $T$ on $\\mathbb R^n$ whose restriction to $U$ equals $t$. The degree of divergence associated with a finite scaling degree $\\delta$ in dimension $n$ is $\\delta-n$. Exact homogeneity of scaling degree $\\delta$ means $\\rho^\\delta T_\\rho=T$ for every $\\rho>0$.\n\nThe definitions use Euclidean dilations about the specified point only. They do not assert that a given distribution arises from a product of fields or that an extension is physically selected."
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-test-frechet-uniform-scaling-order",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Complete test Frechet space and uniform scaling order",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-dependent-choice",
          "def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space",
          "def-qft-distribution-scaling-degree",
          "lem-fixed-support-test-function-spaces-are-complete",
          "thm-baire-category-for-complete-metric-spaces",
          "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-dependent-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-distribution-scaling-degree": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-fixed-support-test-function-spaces-are-complete": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-baire-category-for-complete-metric-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. Brunetti and K. Fredenhagen, Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds, §5.1–§5.2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math-ph/9903028"
            },
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, “Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory,” in Mathematical Aspects of Quantum Field Theories, ed. Damien Calaque and Thomas Strobl, Springer (2015), Section 7",
              "url": "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73875/1/95.pdf.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "K. J. Keller, Dimensional Regularization in Position Space and a Forest Formula for Regularized Epstein-Glaser Renormalization, dissertation, §I.1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1006.2148"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, $U=\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and $t\\in\\mathcal D'(U)$ have finite real scaling degree $\\delta$. For every compact $K\\subset U$ and every real $\\omega>\\delta$, there are an integer $m\\ge0$ and $C<\\infty$ such that\n\n$$|\\langle t_\\rho,\\psi\\rangle|\\le C\\rho^{-\\omega}p_m(\\psi)\\qquad(0<\\rho\\le1,\\ \\psi\\in\\mathcal D_K),$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal D_K$ and $p_m(\\psi)=\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le m}\\sup_{x\\in K}|\\partial^\\alpha\\psi(x)|$ are the fixed-support test-function space and seminorms of [[def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space]]. The estimate is uniform over the entire moving-scale family, not merely over each fixed test separately."
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-finite-scaling-degree-extension",
