          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "R. Brunetti and K. Fredenhagen, Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds, §5.1–§5.2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math-ph/9903028"
            },
            {
              "title": "Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, “Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory,” in Mathematical Aspects of Quantum Field Theories, ed. Damien Calaque and Thomas Strobl, Springer (2015), Section 7",
              "url": "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73875/1/95.pdf.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "K. J. Keller, Dimensional Regularization in Position Space and a Forest Formula for Regularized Epstein-Glaser Renormalization, dissertation, §I.1",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1006.2148"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. On $\\mathbb R^4\\setminus\\{0\\}$ let $u(x)=1/(16\\pi^4|x|^4)$, equivalently the square of the displayed Euclidean kernel $C_0(x)=1/(4\\pi^2|x|^2)$; interpret $u$ as its regular distribution there. Choose $\\chi\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$ equal to one near $0$. For $\\mu>0$ define\n\n$$T_\\mu(\\varphi):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4\\setminus\\{0\\}}\\frac{\\varphi(x)-\\chi(\\mu x)\\varphi(0)}{16\\pi^4|x|^4}\\,d^4x.$$\n\nThen $T_\\mu$ is a distribution extending $u$, has scaling degree $4$, and for every $\\mu,\\nu>0$,\n\n$$T_\\mu-T_\\nu=\\frac{1}{8\\pi^2}\\log(\\mu/\\nu)\\,\\delta_0.$$\n\nIn particular, no extension of $u$ with scaling degree $4$ is exactly homogeneous, where exact homogeneity means $\\rho^4T_\\rho=T$ for every $\\rho>0$. Coordinates use one fixed Euclidean unit; if units are assigned, $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ have reciprocal coordinate units, so the logarithm's ratio is dimensionless. This is a Euclidean distribution identity and does not identify a measured coupling or physical beta function."
      }
