        "generation": {
          "role": "example"
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix $n\\ge1$ and a real $\\delta\\le n$. Define $g_\\delta(x)=|x|^{-\\delta}$ for $x\\ne0$ and choose $g_\\delta(0)=0$; this point value does not affect any integral. On $U=\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\{0\\}$, let $t_\\delta$ be the regular distribution induced by $g_\\delta$. If $\\delta<n$, then $g_\\delta\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^n)$, and its regular extension\n\n$$G_\\delta(\\varphi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}|x|^{-\\delta}\\varphi(x)\\,d^nx$$\n\nhas scaling degree $\\delta$ and is the unique extension of $t_\\delta$ with that same scaling degree. At the critical exponent $\\delta=n$, $t_n$ still has scaling degree $n$ on $U$, but the regular integral diverges logarithmically at $0$; its same-degree extensions exist but form an affine line $T_0+\\mathbb C\\delta_0$."
      }
